=== 4. WHICH BRANCH, scored against section 3 and nothing else ===
  branch A -- cpu0-specific: interrupt affinity or kernel work on cpu0 and its sibling
    4/8 registered row-arms agree
      c0: predicted collapse, 4/4 rows agree
      c5: predicted intact, 0/4 rows agree
      smt=out-of-domain
  branch B -- one physical core is not enough throughput, whichever core it is
    11/12 registered row-arms agree
      c0: predicted collapse, 4/4 rows agree
      c5: predicted collapse, 4/4 rows agree
      smt: predicted collapse, 3/4 rows agree
  branch C -- the Go runtime's own threads contend for the single permitted cpu
    9/12 registered row-arms agree
      c0: predicted collapse, 4/4 rows agree
      c5: predicted collapse, 4/4 rows agree
      smt: predicted intact, 1/4 rows agree

=== VERDICT ===
  NO registered branch is fully consistent with the data.
  That is a result, not a failure: the mechanism is none of the three as stated,
  and the pattern below is what a fourth candidate has to explain.
    branch A: 4/8
    branch B: 11/12
    branch C: 9/12
  indeterminate row-arms: 0 of 12

